# Least Privilege Rules

## Core Principle

Apply the principle of least privilege in all access controls. Grant minimum required access and nothing more.

## Implementation Guidelines

### IAM Policies

- Start with "Deny All" default
- Whitelist only necessary permissions
- Grant fine-grained permissions for specific resources
- Review and audit permissions regularly

### Database Access

- Create database users with minimal required privileges
- Separate read-only and read-write access
- Don't use admin/root accounts for applications

### Application Permissions

- Use role-based access control (RBAC)
- Implement permission checks at the application level
- Verify permissions for every sensitive operation

### Just-in-Time (JIT) Access

- Implement JIT access for privileged operations
- Grant temporary, on-demand access
- Automatically revoke access after use
- Audit all privileged access

## DO

- ✅ Assume "Deny All" default and whitelist permissions
- ✅ Grant only minimum required access
- ✅ Use separate credentials for different environments
- ✅ Implement JIT access for privileged operations
- ✅ Regularly review and audit permissions

## DON'T

- ❌ Grant admin/root access when limited access will do
- ❌ Use overly broad permissions ("*" or "allow all")
- ❌ Share credentials between environments
- ❌ Grant permanent privileged access

## Examples

### Good: Least Privilege IAM Policy

```json
{
  "Effect": "Deny",
  "Action": "*",
  "Resource": "*"
},
{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Action": "s3:GetObject",
  "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/read-only/*"
}
```

### Bad: Overly Permissive Policy

```json
{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Action": "*",
  "Resource": "*"
}
```